window.SKIM_STORY = window.SKIM_STORY || {};
window.SKIM_STORY["gpt-1"] = Object.assign(window.SKIM_STORY["gpt-1"] || {}, {"n-the-mismatch--two-questions": {"id": "n-the-mismatch--two-questions", "parentId": "n-the-mismatch", "parentChapterId": "two-questions", "depth": 2, "title": "What the Two Open Questions Were Made Of", "intro": "The parent named the two obstacles and the machinery each involved. This level opens them up: what each candidate objective actually optimizes, what kind of evidence \"each outperforms the others on different tasks\" is, what each transfer mechanism costs in parameters or tuning, and why the two questions were hard to separate from each other.", "chapters": [{"id": "what-each-objective-optimizes", "title": "What Each Objective Actually Optimizes", "body": "The first question ([[choice-of-pretraining-objective]]) gets one sentence and three citations. It does not say what any of the three optimizes. Here is what each one asks a model to produce.\n\nNext-token prediction is the [[language-modeling-objective]]. Take a run of text, and at every position ask the model for a distribution over the vocabulary. The loss is the log-probability it assigns to the token that actually came next, summed over positions — [[maximum-likelihood-training]] and nothing more. Any text at all is training data for it.\n\nTranslation asks for a whole sentence in a second language given a sentence in the first. The part that gets reused afterwards is the encoder's hidden states, since the decoder half has no job outside translation.\n\nThe paper does not point this out, but that objective needs sentence-aligned pairs, and those pairs are annotated data. So the three candidates do not sit on the same side of [[labeled-data-scarcity]]. Two run on raw text; one runs on a parallel corpus somebody built.\n\nDiscourse coherence asks whether a group of sentences belongs together in the order given. The supervision typically comes free from the document: sentences in their original order are positives, shuffled or substituted ones are negatives. Its usual output is one vector per sentence, which places it near [[phrase-and-sentence-embeddings]].\n\nThat difference in output matters more than the introduction's single sentence suggests. [[word-embedding-transfer]] hands the next model a lookup table. A sentence encoder hands it a vector. A language model hands over every weight in the network — which is what makes [[unsupervised-pretraining]] a choice of starting point rather than a choice of features, and what keeps all of this inside [[semi-supervised-learning-for-nlp]].", "childId": null, "number": "1.2.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 8]}}, {"id": "scatter-not-ranking", "title": "A Scatter, and What Would Resolve It", "body": "Read the claim exactly: each *method* outperforms the others on different tasks. Not each objective. That distinction carries the whole difficulty.\n\nA method is an objective, plus an architecture, plus a corpus, plus a way of moving the result into the target task, plus a choice of which datasets to report. Three groups built three such bundles. When one wins on sentiment and another wins on entailment, the objective is one of five things that differ.\n\nNothing in that comparison isolates it. And the paper does not fix the problem: it never trains a translation-objective or coherence-objective model of its own, so it never runs the head-to-head the sentence describes as unresolved.\n\nWhat it runs instead is a comparison against none. [[ablation-no-pretraining]] in [[table-5]] trains the same transformer directly on the supervised tasks and loses 14.8 points on average. [[layers-transferred]] in [[fig-2]] carries a growing number of pre-trained layers into fine-tuning and finds each one adds. A [[token-level-perplexity]] of 18.4 says the objective was fit well, and [[zero-shot-behaviors]] says the fitted model already performs some of the tasks.\n\nEvery one of those measures pre-training against no pre-training. None measures language modelling against translation. The choice is defended as useful, not as best, which is a weaker claim than the question asked for.\n\nThe footnote is the honest part. It points at the [[glue-benchmark]] leaderboard rather than at a paper. A leaderboard is a number that changes, so citing one as evidence about which objective transfers says the ordering was still moving. That board scores the same [[natural-language-understanding]] tasks the paper later reports on ([[evaluation-task-families]], [[baseline-comparisons]]).", "childId": null, "number": "1.2.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "machinery-and-cost", "title": "Three Kinds of Machinery, Three Kinds of Cost", "body": "[[how-to-transfer-representations]] gets a list of three, each with its own citation. Resolve the citations and the three become concrete.\n\nTask-specific architecture changes first. The transferred representation goes in at the bottom, and above it sits a network shaped around one task: an attention layer between premise and hypothesis for entailment, a pooling scheme for classification, and so on. [[task-specific-architectures]] means these get designed, tuned and trained from scratch, once per task. Neither the design work nor the weights carry to the next task.\n\nIntricate learning schemes second. The citation the introduction attaches here is the same work the related-work section describes as fine-tuning a pre-trained LSTM language model on a staged schedule ([[ulmfit]], inside [[lm-pretraining-then-finetuning-predecessors]] alongside [[semi-supervised-sequence-learning]]).\n\nThe cost there is not parameters. It is schedule: which layers unfreeze in which epoch, a separate learning rate per layer, a shaped rate curve. Those numbers have to be found per task.\n\nExtra unsupervised loss terms third. [[auxiliary-training-objectives]] adds a second loss to the supervised one and optimizes the sum, so raw text keeps shaping the weights while the labels do. The cost is one weight to tune and the compute of a second head.\n\nNow the honest part. The paper does not drop the third mechanism — it keeps it. Fine-tuning here optimizes [[auxiliary-lm-objective]], with [[auxiliary-objective-weight]] set to 0.5, and the paper's own [[ablation-no-auxiliary-objective]] reports the benefit is uneven: larger datasets gain from the term, smaller ones do not.\n\nSo the complaint is not that these mechanisms fail. It is that three were in use at once, each traced to a different paper, and nobody had removed them one at a time. The ablation table later in this paper is what doing so looks like.", "childId": "n-the-mismatch--two-questions--machinery-and-cost", "number": "1.2.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "frozen-features", "title": "Frozen Features, and the Metric the Paper Picks", "body": "A fourth option sits slightly apart from those three, and the paper puts it in the related-work section rather than the introduction.\n\n[[feature-based-transfer]]: freeze the pre-trained model, run the input through it, take the hidden states out, and hand them to a separate task model as extra input vectors alongside that model's own embeddings. The best-known instance is [[elmo-deep-contextualized-word-representations]], where the states come from a pre-trained bidirectional language model.\n\nNote what does not happen. No gradient from the task loss reaches the pre-trained weights. They are a fixed function from tokens to vectors, and everything that learns the task is new.\n\nThat is where [[per-task-parameter-cost]] comes from. The paper's phrase is that such approaches involve a substantial amount of new parameters for each separate target task. It is choosing a metric: count the freshly initialized parameters a new task requires. On that metric its own side reads [[minimal-extra-parameters]] — one [[linear-output-layer-wy]], plus embeddings for the [[delimiter-token]]s that [[task-specific-input-transformations]] introduces through its [[traversal-style-approach]].\n\nThe metric is not neutral. A frozen model has advantages the count does not see. Its states can be computed once and cached across every epoch. No gradients flow through the deep stack. One copy of the pre-trained weights serves all tasks at the same time.\n\nFine-tuning gives up all three. It needs a full backward pass through every layer, and it ends with a separate copy of the entire model per task.\n\nThe paper does not weigh those costs against each other. It picks the parameter count, on which its own approach wins, and moves on.", "childId": null, "number": "1.2.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]}}, {"id": "why-they-compound", "title": "Why the Two Questions Compound", "body": "The paragraph ends with a diagnosis: these two uncertainties are why effective semi-supervised approaches for language had been hard to build. The question is why two open problems should compound rather than simply add.\n\nThey compound because the objective constrains the transfer. A translation model's usable half is an encoder, so its output arrives as features and a task network has to be built to consume them. A sentence encoder hands over one vector per sentence, and anything the task needs below that level must come from elsewhere.\n\nA language model is the case where the whole stack comes across. That is the one setting in which [[transfer-learning]] can mean keeping every weight and continuing to train it.\n\nThe citations show the tangle directly. The work cited for the language modelling objective is also cited for task-specific architecture changes. The work cited for translation is also cited as a source of auxiliary features. Nobody was varying one factor at a time, because each published system was a single point rather than a row in a grid.\n\nThat shape explains what the rest of the paper does. It fixes one objective and one transfer style — [[generative-pretraining]] followed by fine-tuning, together the [[two-stage-training-procedure]] — and then holds the architecture constant across every task, which is what [[task-agnostic-model]] means here.\n\nIts argument against recurrent predecessors ([[lstm-short-range-limitation]]) and its count of [[state-of-the-art-9-of-12]] are both attempts to make one point in that grid carry the case. Neither closes the comparison the introduction says is open. They argue that this pairing works, not that it is the right one.", "childId": null, "number": "1.2.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "1.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-which-family-this-joins--closest-line": {"id": "n-which-family-this-joins--closest-line", "parentId": "n-which-family-this-joins", "parentChapterId": "closest-line", "depth": 2, "title": "Nearly the Same Recipe", "intro": "The parent gave you the shape of the argument: same recipe, two claimed differences, one of them barely measured. This level opens each piece up — what the two earlier systems actually trained, what the short-range sentence is asserting step by step, and how much weight each experiment can carry once you look at what it varied.", "chapters": [{"id": "what-they-did", "title": "What the Two Predecessors Actually Trained", "body": "[[semi-supervised-sequence-learning]] is the earlier of the two works named under [[lm-pretraining-then-finetuning-predecessors]], and its pre-training stage came in two forms.\n\nOne was the [[language-modeling-objective]]: read unlabelled text and predict the next token. The other was a sequence autoencoder: read a sequence into a recurrent state, then train the network to emit that same sequence back out. Either way the pre-trained weights were those of an [[lstm-gpt1]], and either way the second stage was [[supervised-fine-tuning|supervised fine-tuning]] of the whole network for [[text-classification]].\n\n[[ulmfit]] kept only the language modelling half. It pre-trained an LSTM language model, then fine-tuned it for classification on a schedule that was staged rather than uniform — different layers brought in at different points, with the learning rate shaped to match.\n\nThat staging is worth holding onto, because this paper cites the same work twice for two different reasons.\n\nIn the related work section it appears as the closest line. In the introduction it appears in the list of things that make [[how-to-transfer-representations]] an open question, under the heading of intricate learning schemes.\n\nSo the paper is not only claiming a different architecture. It is also claiming that its own second stage is plainer than ULMFiT's: [[three-epochs-fine-tuning]] for most datasets, and [[minimal-extra-parameters]] on top of the pre-trained stack.\n\nThat difference is never argued in the related work section. It is stated as a property of [[two-stage-training-procedure]] elsewhere, and left there. Only [[unsupervised-pretraining]] as a category, and the range claim, are put forward here as the separation.", "childId": null, "number": "2.5.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "inside-the-sentence", "title": "One Sentence, and the Chain Inside It", "body": "[[lstm-short-range-limitation]] is a single clause: the predecessors' use of LSTMs restricts their prediction ability to a short range. Unpacked, it is two steps, and only the second is about transfer.\n\nThe first step is about what a recurrent network can learn at all. Everything it knows about the prefix lives in one fixed-size state, rewritten at every token. A dependency reaching back four hundred tokens has to survive four hundred overwrites of that state to affect the prediction.\n\nThe second step is the transfer consequence. If pre-training never taught the model to condition on such a dependency, the pre-trained weights hold nothing about it to carry into the target task. Whatever linguistic information the phase does capture, the reach of the transferred model is capped by the reach of the pre-trained one.\n\nThe paper's alternative is stated as a property of [[multi-layer-transformer-decoder]]. [[masked-self-attention]] lets every position take a direct weighted read of every earlier position, with no compression through time in between, and [[context-window-512-tokens]] fixes how far back that reach extends.\n\nThe corpus is chosen so the window has something to reach across. [[bookscorpus]] supplies [[long-range-contiguous-text]] — sentences and paragraphs in their original order — where the [[one-billion-word-benchmark]] used by [[elmo-deep-contextualized-word-representations]] is shuffled at the sentence level.\n\nWhat is never supplied is a number. The paper does not say what range an LSTM reaches, nor what range its own model reaches, nor how [[long-range-dependencies-gpt1]] such as [[coreference]] across sentences fare under either. The sentence ends with a forward pointer to the experiments.", "childId": null, "number": "2.5.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "the-one-swap", "title": "The One Controlled Swap, and What It Varied", "body": "Of the three rows in [[ablation-studies]], reported in [[table-5]], one speaks to the range claim. [[ablation-lstm-substitution]] holds the framework fixed — same input transformations, same fine-tuning procedure, same objective — and replaces the transformer with a single-layer 2048-unit LSTM. The average score falls by 5.6 points.\n\nRead that configuration against [[model-specification]]. The transformer is [[twelve-layer-depth]] with [[hidden-state-dimension-768]] states. The LSTM is one layer, of 2048 units.\n\nSo the swap changes two things at once: recurrence for attention, and depth twelve for depth one. The paper's own layer analysis says depth carries value, which means part of the 5.6 may be depth rather than range.\n\nThe exception is [[mrpc]], the [[semantic-similarity]] dataset built from news sentence pairs, and the only dataset where the LSTM comes out ahead. The paper reports the exception in one clause and does not account for it. The pairs there are short, which is where a limit on reach should cost least — but the paper does not say that, and neither does the table.\n\nFor scale, the same table reports [[ablation-no-pretraining]] at 14.8 points. Architecture is worth roughly a third of what stage one is worth, on the paper's own averaging, while [[ablation-no-auxiliary-objective]] moves the average by far less than either.\n\nAll of which leaves 5.6 as a scalar summarising a set of dataset scores. Nothing in it is indexed by distance, so nothing in it separates a range effect from any other advantage the transformer might have.", "childId": null, "number": "2.5.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "layers-and-variance", "title": "Two Diagnostics That Point Sideways", "body": "[[layers-transferred]] is the left panel of [[fig-2]]. Dev accuracy on [[multinli]] and [[race-dataset]] is plotted against how many pre-trained blocks are carried into fine-tuning. Transferring the embeddings alone already helps, and each further block adds more, reaching up to 9% for full transfer on MultiNLI.\n\nThe conclusion drawn is that every layer holds function worth carrying. That is a claim about how much of the stack transfers.\n\nIt is not a claim about how far back a prediction reaches. A curve rising with depth would look the same whether the extra layers bought range, abstraction, or capacity.\n\nThe right panel tracks [[zero-shot-behaviors]]: four heuristics, three of them built on [[average-token-log-probability-scoring]], run against the generative model across pre-training checkpoints with no fine-tuning at all. The scores rise steadily and stay stable.\n\n[[transformer-inductive-bias]] is the hypothesis attached to that panel — that attention gives a more structured memory than a recurrent state, and that this is what assists transfer. The evidence offered for it is one observation: the LSTM shows higher variance in its zero-shot scores.\n\nHigher variance across checkpoints is instability. It is not reach.\n\nThe paper's own verb is suggesting, twice in that paragraph, and the hedge is fair. Put together, the range claim rests on an averaged score gap, a depth curve, a variance observation, and a design argument about a 512-token window over contiguous book text. Each is consistent with the claim. None of them measures it.", "childId": null, "number": "2.5.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "breadth-by-inspection", "title": "The Difference You Can Check by Inspection", "body": "The second claimed difference asks nothing of the reader beyond looking at [[table-1]]. [[evaluation-task-families]] covers four families and twelve datasets, where the predecessors reported [[text-classification]].\n\nWalk the list. Five datasets are [[natural-language-inference]], from [[snli]] at roughly 550k training examples down to [[rte]] at 2490. Two are [[question-answering-and-commonsense-reasoning]], including the [[story-cloze-test]], where a multi-sentence story has to be finished with the right one of two endings. Three are [[semantic-similarity]], among them [[qqp]] and [[sts-b]] at about 5.7k examples. Two are single-sentence classification, one of which is [[sst-2]].\n\nSo ten of the twelve sit outside the task type the earlier work reported on. Only the last two are the same kind of problem.\n\nThat is a fact about which datasets were run, not an inference from a score gap. It survives whatever you conclude about range.\n\nAnd it is carried by a different part of the paper. Breadth comes from [[task-agnostic-model]] plus the input transformations that fold ordered pairs and document-question-answer triples into one token sequence. Nothing in that argument depends on choosing attention over recurrence.\n\nThe headline that follows — [[state-of-the-art-9-of-12]] — is likewise a count, not a mechanism.\n\nWhich leaves the two claimed differences resting on very different footings. One is asserted, pointed forward, and then supported indirectly. The other is simply exhibited.", "childId": null, "number": "2.5.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "2.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-predicting-the-next-token--stack-dimensions": {"id": "n-predicting-the-next-token--stack-dimensions", "parentId": "n-predicting-the-next-token", "parentChapterId": "stack-dimensions", "depth": 2, "title": "The Numbers the Stack Was Built With", "intro": "The parent listed the dimensions. This level takes them one at a time and asks what each one actually controls inside a block, what it costs in weights or activations, and what the paper offers as a reason for it — which, for most of them, is nothing.", "chapters": [{"id": "one-width-everywhere", "title": "The Width That Nothing Is Allowed to Change", "body": "Of the numbers in [[model-specification]], 768 is the least free. It is not a per-layer setting that happens to be held constant. Three separate places in [[multi-layer-transformer-decoder]] pin it to the same value at once.\n\nThe first is the input. [[input-representation-h0]] adds token embeddings and position embeddings together, so both tables must produce vectors of the same width, and that width becomes the width of everything downstream.\n\nThe second is the residual stream. Each block adds its sublayer outputs back into the state it received, so whatever a block computes internally, it must hand back something of the same shape it was given.\n\nThe third is the output. [[output-softmax-tied-embeddings]] scores the vocabulary by multiplying the top state by the transpose of the embedding table. That product only makes sense if a top-layer state is the same length as one row of [[token-embedding-matrix]].\n\nSo [[hidden-state-dimension-768]] is one decision made once, not twelve.\n\nIt is also where a large block of weights sits outside the stack. [[byte-pair-encoding-gpt1]] with [[bpe-merges-40000]] gives a vocabulary of roughly forty thousand entries. Forty thousand rows of 768 numbers is 30.7M weights. [[learned-position-embeddings]] add one row per slot in the [[context-window-512-tokens]], so 512 by 768, about 0.4M more.\n\nTying means that table is read twice per forward pass — once to look up, once transposed to score — and receives gradient from both.", "childId": null, "number": "4.4.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}], "pages": [3, 4, 5]}}, {"id": "heads-that-cost-nothing", "title": "Twelve Heads, and What They Do Not Cost", "body": "Head count is the one dimension here that is free in parameters.\n\nA block's attention uses four square projections — query, key, value, and the output projection that recombines. Each is 768 by 768, whatever the head count. [[twelve-attention-heads]] does not add a thirteenth matrix; it slices the three input projections into twelve strips of 768 by 64 and lets each strip run its own attention. The recombining matrix then stitches the twelve 64-dimensional results back into 768. Four times 589,824 is about 2.4M weights, and that figure is the same for one head or for twelve.\n\nWhat the count does change is how many distinct attention patterns a block can hold. One head must fold all its evidence about the past into a single softmax over positions. Twelve can keep twelve, which matters for the [[long-range-dependencies-gpt1]] the paper is after, where one head might track the nearest few tokens while another reaches back hundreds.\n\nThe 64-dimensional subspace also sets the scale inside the softmax: dot products are divided by $\\sqrt{64}$, which is 8.\n\nThe real cost is activations. Under [[masked-self-attention]], each head builds a triangular 512 by 512 score matrix; twelve heads is about 3.1M scores per sequence per block, and [[pretraining-schedule]] runs 64 sequences at a time.\n\nOn the choice itself, the paper says only that it largely follows the original [[transformer-gpt1]] work. [[eq-decoder-stack]] treats the block as given, and [[decoder-only-stack]] inherits its interior unchanged. No head-count comparison is reported.", "childId": null, "number": "4.4.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8]}}, {"id": "fourfold-and-the-bend", "title": "The Sublayer That Holds Two Thirds of the Weights", "body": "The second sublayer is where most of a block's parameters live, and where none of its communication happens.\n\n\"Position-wise\" in [[position-wise-feedforward-3072]] means the same 768 to 3072 to 768 map runs at every position independently, with nothing passing sideways between them. That splits the block cleanly: attention moves information between tokens, and this sublayer does the per-token computation on what arrived.\n\nCount it. $768 \\times 3072$ is 2,359,296, and there are two such matrices, up and back down. About 4.7M, against attention's 2.4M. Two thirds of each block.\n\nBetween the two matrices sits [[gelu]], written $x\\,\\Phi(x)$, where $\\Phi$ is the standard normal cumulative distribution. Work two inputs through it. At $x = 2$, $\\Phi$ is about 0.977, so the input passes through nearly untouched. At $x = -2$, $\\Phi$ is about 0.023, so the output is about $-0.046$ — close to zero, but not zero, and still carrying gradient.\n\nThat is the difference from a hard cut at zero. The curve also dips slightly below zero for mildly negative inputs before flattening, so it is not monotone.\n\nThe paper states the choice with a citation and no comparison. No ReLU control run appears anywhere in it.\n\nOne more thing acts on this sublayer's output. Under [[regularization-setup]], [[residual-embedding-attention-dropout]] applies [[dropout-gpt1]] at rate 0.1 to residual branches, so what a block computes is thinned before it is added back. [[fig-1]] draws the two sublayers in sequence.", "childId": null, "number": "4.4.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}], "pages": [3, 4, 5]}}, {"id": "init-and-layernorm", "title": "One Standard Deviation for Every Matrix", "body": "[[weight-initialization]] draws every weight from $\\mathcal{N}(0, 0.02)$. The thing to notice is that this is one number applied to matrices with very different fan-in.\n\nCompare it against the usual scaling. For a projection reading 768 inputs, $1/\\sqrt{768}$ is about 0.036, so 0.02 is a little over half of what a fan-in rule would ask. For the feed-forward matrix reading 3072 inputs, $1/\\sqrt{3072}$ is about 0.018, and 0.02 is almost exactly it.\n\nSo the same draw is roughly fan-in-scaled for the widest matrix in the block and deliberately small for everything else.\n\nThe paper's stated reason for getting away with this is [[layer-normalization-gpt1]]. Normalization rescales each position's activations to zero mean and unit variance before applying a learned gain and bias, so the size of what reaches the next matrix is largely reset regardless of how the weights were drawn. The word the paper uses is \"sufficient.\"\n\nThat is an assertion, not a result. No initialization sweep is reported, no run that diverged under an alternative, no comparison of any kind.\n\nThe prose also does not say where normalization sits within a block, only that it is used extensively throughout the model; the block diagram shows one after each sublayer.\n\nThose learned gains surface again in [[pre-training-optimization]]. The [[decoupled-weight-decay]] term applies $w = 0.01$ to all non-bias and non-gain weights, which exempts them. Together with [[adam-optimizer-gpt1]] and the [[linear-warmup]] over 2000 updates, this is the whole of what keeps early training stable, and none of it is ablated.", "childId": null, "number": "4.4.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}], "pages": [3, 4, 5]}}, {"id": "depth-as-dial", "title": "The Only Number the Paper Turns", "body": "Every dimension so far arrives as a stated setting with nothing behind it. [[twelve-layer-depth]] is different, because [[layers-transferred]] makes it a variable.\n\nThe left panel of [[fig-2]] plots dev accuracy on [[multinli]] and [[race-dataset]] against how many of the twelve pre-trained blocks are carried into fine-tuning. The leftmost point transfers the embedding table alone, which is [[word-embedding-transfer]], and which the paper calls the standard result. From there, accuracy climbs with each block added, reaching up to 9% for full transfer on MultiNLI.\n\nRead what that licenses. The curve is still rising at twelve, and the paper reports no point past it and no run at any other depth.\n\nSo the sweep says no transferred layer was dead weight. It does not say twelve was the right depth, and it does not show the gain flattening out.\n\nThere is a price attached to the 9%. At roughly 7.1M weights per block, carrying all twelve moves about 85M parameters into the target task, on top of the embedding table.\n\nOne nearby number is easy to misread as the same experiment. In [[table-5]], [[ablation-no-pretraining]] costs 14.8 points — but that is the same twelve-layer stack trained on the task from scratch, not the zero-layer end of this curve.\n\nThe evidence here is two datasets, dev accuracy, single runs, no error bars.", "childId": null, "number": "4.4.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "4.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-the-corpus-and-the-training-run--shape-of-the-stack": {"id": "n-the-corpus-and-the-training-run--shape-of-the-stack", "parentId": "n-the-corpus-and-the-training-run", "parentChapterId": "shape-of-the-stack", "depth": 2, "title": "How the Dimensions Fit Together", "intro": "One block, followed from the vector that enters it to the vector that leaves. Then the arithmetic behind the smaller choices the specification states in a sentence each: what each one costs, what it leaves undefined, and how much the paper actually tested.", "chapters": [{"id": "inside-one-block", "title": "One Block, Followed Through", "body": "A single position enters a block carrying one [[hidden-state-dimension-768|768-number]] vector. [[input-representation-h0|At layer zero]] that vector is a token embedding plus a position embedding. Above layer zero it is whatever the block below produced.\n\nThe attention sublayer first makes three versions of it. The query, key and value projections each multiply the vector by a $768 \\times 768$ matrix, so all three come out at full width.\n\nThen the split. Those 768 numbers are cut into [[twelve-attention-heads|twelve]] contiguous slices of 64. Head $h$ takes slice $h$ of the query, slice $h$ of the key and slice $h$ of the value, and never sees the other eleven.\n\nNothing is duplicated by this. Twelve heads of width 64 cost exactly the arithmetic of one head of width 768. What changes is that the comparison between two tokens becomes twelve separate 64-dimensional dot products rather than one 768-dimensional one — twelve narrow views instead of one wide one.\n\nThe head width also fixes the score scale. Attention divides each dot product by $\\sqrt{d_k}$, and $d_k = 64$ makes that divisor exactly 8.\n\nEach head's scores then pass the [[masked-self-attention|causal mask]] before the softmax, so positions after the current one get zero weight. There is no encoder output to attend to instead — [[decoder-only-stack|that half is gone]].\n\nThe twelve 64-dimensional results are concatenated back to 768 and sent through the fourth $768 \\times 768$ matrix.\n\nThe second sublayer mixes nothing. [[position-wise-feedforward-3072|The feed-forward network]] runs on each position on its own: up to 3072, activation, back down to 768.\n\nBoth sublayers sit on residual branches with [[layer-normalization-gpt1|layer normalization]]. This is the block [[fig-1|Figure 1 draws]] once, stacked twelve times, and written out in [[eq-decoder-stack|Equation 2]] — the [[transformer-gpt1|transformer]] block with the encoder attention removed.", "childId": "n-the-corpus-and-the-training-run--shape-of-the-stack--inside-one-block", "number": "5.3.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8]}}, {"id": "where-the-count-lands", "title": "Where the Count Actually Lands", "body": "The total is worth splitting three ways, because the three parts behave differently.\n\nInside a block the two sublayers are not equal. Attention's four projections come to $4 \\times 589{,}824$. The feed-forward pair comes to $2 \\times 2{,}359{,}296$, which is that same quantity twice over. So exactly two thirds of a block's weights sit in the position-wise map and one third in attention.\n\nMost of the per-block capacity is the transformation applied after mixing, not the mixing.\n\nThe embedding table is the other large piece. [[bpe-merges-40000|40,000 merges]] is not itself a row count: the vocabulary is the base symbols, plus the merged symbols, plus the tokens added later for fine-tuning, and the paper never states the total. Round to 40,000 rows and the table is $40{,}000 \\times 768 = 30{,}720{,}000$ parameters.\n\nThat table is used at both ends. [[token-embedding-matrix|$W_e$]] maps tokens in at the bottom, and [[output-softmax-tied-embeddings|its transpose scores the vocabulary]] at the top. There is no separate output matrix, so those rows take gradient from the input lookup and from the final softmax together.\n\nThen there is a cost no parameter count shows. Each head builds a score matrix over the [[context-window-512-tokens|512-token window]]: $512 \\times 512 = 262{,}144$ numbers. Twelve heads across [[twelve-layer-depth|twelve layers]] make $37{,}748{,}736$ of them for one sequence, and a minibatch holds 64 sequences — about 2.4 billion attention weights alive per step.\n\nIn arithmetic it is smaller than that sounds. Per token per block, the four projections are 2.36M multiply-accumulates, the feed-forward 4.72M, and the attend-and-average over 512 positions about 0.79M. At this window the quadratic term is roughly a tenth of the work. The window costs memory more than it costs compute, and none of this appears in [[model-specification|the specification]].", "childId": null, "number": "5.3.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "positions-as-a-table", "title": "A Table Instead of a Function", "body": "[[learned-position-embeddings|The switch to a learned table]] gets one sentence in the paper and no reason attached to it.\n\nThe two options are different kinds of object. A sinusoid is a function of the slot index and the channel: hand it any integer and it returns a vector. Slot 900 has an encoding whether or not any training sequence ever reached slot 900.\n\nA table is a parameter. $W_p$ has 512 rows because the model reads 512 slots. Row 513 is not wrong or degraded. It does not exist.\n\nThe window has stopped being a training decision and become a shape.\n\nIn parameters it is cheap: $512 \\times 768 = 393{,}216$, under half a percent of the stack.\n\nThe interesting difference is in training traffic, since a learned row only knows what gradient reached it. [[pretraining-schedule|Every pre-training minibatch]] is 64 contiguous sequences of exactly 512 tokens. Every one of the 512 rows therefore appears once in every sequence, at every update. The last row is trained as hard as the first.\n\nStage two does not hold that property. [[task-specific-input-transformations|Task inputs are rewritten]] into a single sequence starting at slot one, bracketed by [[start-and-end-tokens|start and end tokens]] and split by a [[delimiter-token|delimiter]]. Many of those sequences stop well short of 512 slots, and fine-tuning runs for [[three-epochs-fine-tuning|three passes]] over a small dataset.\n\nSo the high-index rows enter each task carrying only what pre-training put there, and receive little further gradient.\n\nThe paper measures none of this. It reports no comparison of learned against sinusoidal embeddings, and no evidence that the substitution changed any result.", "childId": null, "number": "5.3.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]}}, {"id": "the-smooth-gate", "title": "A Gate That Slides", "body": "[[gelu|GELU]] and ReLU differ in exactly one factor. ReLU multiplies $x$ by the indicator $\\mathbb{1}[x>0]$, a gate that is either 0 or 1. GELU multiplies $x$ by $\\Phi(x)$, a gate that slides continuously between them.\n\nPut two numbers through it. At $x = 2$, $\\Phi(2) = 0.977$ and the output is $1.95$, where ReLU would give $2$. At $x = -2$, $\\Phi(-2) = 0.023$ and the output is $-0.046$, where ReLU would give exactly $0$.\n\nTwo things follow from that.\n\nFirst, there is no flat half-line. ReLU's derivative is identically zero for every negative input, so a unit driven negative receives no gradient at all. GELU's derivative is $\\Phi(x) + x\\phi(x)$, which shrinks as $x$ falls but is never zero across a stretch.\n\nSecond, the function is not monotone. That derivative crosses zero near $x = -0.75$, where GELU bottoms out around $-0.17$ and then climbs back toward zero. Slightly more negative inputs can produce less negative outputs.\n\nAll of this happens inside the feed-forward sublayer, between its two matrices.\n\nThe evidence behind the choice is thin, and the paper does not pretend otherwise. It names GELU with a citation and says nothing more. It is not among [[ablation-studies|the three removals]] the paper does run — [[ablation-no-pretraining|no pre-training]], [[ablation-lstm-substitution|an LSTM in place of the transformer]], and [[ablation-no-auxiliary-objective|no auxiliary loss]] — reported in [[table-5|Table 5]]. Nothing here says what the same stack with ReLU would have scored.", "childId": null, "number": "5.3.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "why-small-init-held", "title": "Why 0.02 Was Enough", "body": "The claim attached to [[weight-initialization|$\\mathcal{N}(0, 0.02)$]] is that layernorm made it sufficient. It reads better once you see what would otherwise have gone wrong.\n\nTake a $768 \\times 768$ projection whose input components have unit variance. Draw the weights at standard deviation $\\sigma$ and the outputs come out at $\\sigma\\sqrt{768}$, which is $\\sigma \\times 27.7$.\n\nFor that to land at 1, $\\sigma$ would have to be $1/\\sqrt{768} = 0.036$. The rule usually paired with rectified activations asks for $\\sqrt{2/768} = 0.051$. At $0.02$ the output standard deviation is $0.554$ instead.\n\nThat is a shrink of nearly half at every matrix. There are two matrix stages per block and twelve blocks in [[multi-layer-transformer-decoder|the stack]]. Compounded, $0.554^{24}$ is about $7 \\times 10^{-7}$ — the signal would reach the top essentially gone, and the gradient would come back the same way.\n\nNormalization removes the compounding. Each normalized point rescales its vector to zero mean and unit variance across the 768 channels, so the shrink is undone before the next stage and absolute scale never accumulates.\n\nWhat the small draw then controls is not activation scale. It is how large each sublayer's contribution is against the residual stream it is added to, and at step zero that is small — further attenuated by [[residual-embedding-attention-dropout|dropout on the residual branches]].\n\nOne related detail sits in the same paragraph of the paper: [[decoupled-weight-decay|the modified weight decay]] skips all bias and gain weights, leaving layernorm's learned scales free.\n\nThe same 0.02 covers every matrix, with no per-layer adjustment — which is what makes it simple. And \"sufficient\" is a report that the run worked, not a comparison. No initialization experiment appears in [[pre-training-optimization|the training setup]] or the analysis.", "childId": null, "number": "5.3.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}], "pages": [3, 4, 5]}}], "number": "5.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, "n-from-language-model-to-classifier--why-the-old-loss-stays-on": {"id": "n-from-language-model-to-classifier--why-the-old-loss-stays-on", "parentId": "n-from-language-model-to-classifier", "parentChapterId": "why-the-old-loss-stays-on", "depth": 2, "title": "The Second Loss, Term by Term", "intro": "The parent settled what the auxiliary term is and where the paper puts it. This level does the arithmetic: which text the loss actually reads, what the tied head saves and what it costs to run, what $\\lambda = 0.5$ does and does not pin down, how thin the evidence for the two claimed effects is, and what the ablation row comes to when you do the subtraction yourself.", "chapters": [{"id": "what-the-loss-reads", "title": "Which Text the Loss Reads", "body": "$\\mathcal{C}$ — the [[labeled-dataset-c]] — does not hold plain sentences by the time either loss touches it. Its inputs have already been through [[task-specific-input-transformations]].\n\nSo the sequence the auxiliary term scores is the transformed one, and that sequence contains tokens no book contains. Every input is bracketed by [[start-and-end-tokens]]. The parts inside are separated by the [[delimiter-token]] `$`.\n\n[[eq-lm-objective]] asks the model to predict each token from the tokens before it, and these markers sit in the stream like any other token. The paper does not say whether the positions holding them are scored or skipped.\n\nHow many sequences an example produces also varies by task. [[entailment-input-transformation]] makes one sequence from a premise and a hypothesis. [[similarity-input-transformation]] makes two, one for each ordering of the pair. [[qa-and-commonsense-input-transformation]] makes one per candidate answer, so a four-way multiple-choice question yields four.\n\nWhatever the label loss reads, the auxiliary term reads exactly the same sequences.\n\nWhat it never reads is the label. $y$ is not in the token stream at all; it is reachable only through [[linear-output-layer-wy]]. So the gradient from the auxiliary term flows into the embeddings and into every transformer block, and never into $W_y$.\n\nThere is a shift in the text itself as well. Pre-training scored [[long-range-contiguous-text]] in windows of [[context-window-512-tokens]]. A premise, a `$` and a hypothesis is a much shorter object with a marker in the middle.\n\nThe same objective is now being maximized on text of a different shape, and the paper does not comment on that.", "childId": null, "number": "6.3.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8]}}, {"id": "free-in-parameters", "title": "Free in Parameters, Not in Compute", "body": "\"No new parameters\" is worth putting a number on.\n\nThe vocabulary comes from [[byte-pair-encoding-gpt1]] built with [[bpe-merges-40000]], so it holds on the order of 40,000 subword units. Each state in the stack is [[hidden-state-dimension-768]] wide. A projection from a 768-dimensional state to 40,000 scores is therefore a matrix of about 30.7 million entries.\n\n[[output-softmax-tied-embeddings]] means that matrix is never stored. The head is the transpose of [[token-embedding-matrix]], which the input side needs anyway.\n\nFor scale: each of the [[twelve-layer-depth]] blocks holds roughly 7 million weights, so twelve of them come to around 85 million. An untied output projection would have added about a third of the stack again, carried through pre-training and into every fine-tuning run.\n\nThat is what the count in [[minimal-extra-parameters]] — one matrix $W_y$, plus embeddings for delimiters — quietly rests on. It is also the same measure the paper uses against [[per-task-parameter-cost]] elsewhere.\n\nParameters are not the only cost. $W_y$ is applied once per sequence, at [[final-token-activation]]. The tied head is applied at every position, and each application is those 30.7 million multiply-adds followed by a softmax over 40,000 rows.\n\nA pass through all twelve blocks at one position is around 85 million multiply-adds, before counting attention. So scoring every position with the language modelling head adds roughly a third to the forward work of a fine-tuning step.\n\nUnder [[three-epochs-fine-tuning]] at [[fine-tuning-lr-and-batch]] that is a bounded amount of extra compute. The paper reports no timing for either configuration.", "childId": null, "number": "6.3.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "what-lambda-balances", "title": "What $\\lambda = 0.5$ Balances", "body": "Both terms in [[eq-combined-objective]] are written as sums, and they sum over different things.\n\n[[supervised-objective-l2]], as written in [[eq-supervised-objective]], contributes one log-probability per labelled example: the log-probability of that example's correct label. [[language-modeling-objective]], applied to the same example, contributes one log-probability per token.\n\nTake a premise-hypothesis pair forty tokens long. The label side of the sum has one term in it. The auxiliary side has forty. Scaling by [[auxiliary-objective-weight]] at $0.5$ leaves twenty against one.\n\nSo $0.5$ does not, on its own, mean the two objectives are weighted evenly. Where the ratio actually lands depends on whether an implementation sums or averages each term, and on how long the task's sequences are. The paper writes sums, and does not say which its code does.\n\nOne consequence is that a single $\\lambda$ means different things across the twelve datasets. A [[race-dataset]] example is a passage, a question and a candidate answer. A [[cola]] example is one sentence. At the same $\\lambda$, the auxiliary term carries far more of the gradient in the first case than in the second.\n\n$\\lambda$ is reported once, in the closing line of [[fine-tuning-hyperparameters]], in the same list as the learning rate, the batch size and the [[classifier-dropout]]. One value covers every task. No search over it is reported, and no sensitivity is measured.\n\nThat matters more here than it would for a well-behaved knob. The ablation ahead reports the term's effect changing sign from dataset to dataset, which makes a per-task $\\lambda$ the obvious response — and it is not attempted.", "childId": null, "number": "6.3.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "two-claims", "title": "Two Claims, Unequally Evidenced", "body": "The paper's case for keeping the term is one sentence: including language modelling as an auxiliary objective helped by improving generalization of the supervised model, and by accelerating convergence. Two references follow, cited as prior work that saw the same.\n\nThe two claims are not evidenced the same way, so take them apart.\n\nGeneralization is testable inside this paper. Train with the term, train without it, compare held-out scores. That experiment exists — it is [[ablation-no-auxiliary-objective]], one of the three in [[ablation-studies]] — and the next chapter reads it.\n\nConvergence has nothing measuring it. No fine-tuning learning curve appears anywhere in the paper. [[fig-2]] holds the only training-progress plots, and both concern stage one: the left panel is [[layers-transferred]], the right tracks [[zero-shot-behaviors]] across pre-training updates.\n\nNeither says how many fine-tuning steps a run took with the term compared to without. The claim is stated and left there.\n\nNo mechanism is offered for either effect. The paper does not say whether the term acts as a regularizer, keeps the tied head calibrated on text of the new shape, or does something else. [[pretraining-as-regularization]] is raised in the related work, but about stage one, not about this.\n\nThe cited corroboration also sits at a slant. The clearer of the two references, named under [[auxiliary-training-objectives]], added a language modelling loss to sequence labelling — predicting a tag at every token, not one label from the last position.\n\nAnd the paper's own reading of that branch is that [[generative-pretraining]] already learns several of the linguistic aspects such an objective would supply. That is an argument for the term mattering less here than it did there.", "childId": null, "number": "6.3.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "the-ablation-row", "title": "Doing the Subtraction Yourself", "body": "[[table-5]] carries a row labelled \"Transformer w/o aux LM\". It is the same recipe with $\\lambda$ set to zero.\n\nStart with what it does not cover. The table reports eight datasets, not twelve. [[snli]], [[scitail]], RACE and the [[story-cloze-test]] are absent — so the term's effect on the two benchmarks carrying the paper's largest reported margins is never measured at all.\n\nNow the eight. Keeping the term costs 2.5 points on CoLA (45.4 against 47.9), 0.7 on [[sst-2]] (91.3 against 92.0), 2.6 on [[mrpc]] (82.3 against 84.9) and 1.2 on [[sts-b]] (82.0 against 83.2).\n\nIt gains 0.5 on [[qqp]] (70.3 against 69.8), 0.7 on [[multinli]] (81.8 against 81.1), 1.2 on [[qnli]] (88.1 against 86.9) and 1.6 on [[rte]] (56.0 against 54.4).\n\nThe losses total 7.0 points. The gains total 4.0. Net $-3.0$ across eight datasets is $-0.375$ each, which rounds to the gap between the two reported averages, 74.7 and 75.0.\n\nThat average is unweighted, and the caption says so. It adds one Matthews correlation, four accuracies, two F1 scores and one Pearson correlation as though they shared a unit. CoLA's 2.5-point Matthews swing counts exactly as much as QQP's 0.5-point F1 swing.\n\nThen the size reading. RTE is a dataset the paper itself calls one of the smaller ones, at 2490 examples, and it shows the largest single gain from keeping the term.\n\nSTS-B, at roughly 5.7k examples, is hurt by it and fits the trend. Those two are the only ones of the eight whose sizes the paper prints.", "childId": null, "number": "6.3.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "6.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-one-sequence-per-task--the-four-shapes": {"id": "n-one-sequence-per-task--the-four-shapes", "parentId": "n-one-sequence-per-task", "parentChapterId": "the-four-shapes", "depth": 2, "title": "Four Shapes, and What Each Costs to Run", "intro": "The parent priced the four templates. This level takes each apart at the level of the mechanism: what the markers actually add in the cases that look free, why the similarity merge is addition rather than something else, what the softmax over candidates is normalizing, and where the running bill lands against a window the paper never budgets for.", "chapters": [{"id": "markers-only", "title": "The Two Templates That Only Add Markers", "body": "Calling the classification case untransformed is nearly right, and the exception is worth having. The paper says all transformations include adding randomly initialized start and end tokens, $\\langle s\\rangle$ and $\\langle e\\rangle$ ([[start-and-end-tokens]]).\n\nSo a single sentence going into [[text-classification]] is still rewritten. Two positions are bracketed onto it, and both carry embeddings that did not exist during pre-training.\n\nThose embeddings are learned during fine-tuning. They belong to the small set of freshly initialized parameters that a new task brings with it ([[minimal-extra-parameters]]).\n\nEntailment adds one more symbol. The [[delimiter-token]] `$` goes between the premise and the hypothesis ([[entailment-input-transformation]]), so the sequence has five parts: start, premise, delimiter, hypothesis, end. One forward pass, over a sequence of length $|p| + |h| + 3$.\n\nThe order in that sequence is not a choice anyone made. [[natural-language-inference]] hands over a directed pair: the premise is given, and the hypothesis is the thing being judged against it.\n\nThat direction interacts with the stack in a way the paper does not comment on. Under [[masked-self-attention]] each position attends only to itself and to earlier positions.\n\nHypothesis tokens can therefore attend back into the premise. Premise tokens cannot see the hypothesis at all.\n\nThe premise is read once, without knowing what will be asked of it. Only the hypothesis is read in the light of the other sentence.\n\n[[fig-1]] draws these two panels as nearly the same picture: one text block for classification, two blocks and a delimiter for entailment, each feeding one transformer and one linear head. That visual sameness is the [[traversal-style-approach]] working on its easy cases, where [[structured-inputs]] already carry a reading order and only need writing down in it.", "childId": null, "number": "7.3.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "symmetry-by-addition", "title": "Buying Symmetry with a Second Pass", "body": "The repair fits in one sentence of the paper: build both orderings, process each independently, add the two representations element-wise, feed the sum to the linear layer ([[similarity-input-transformation]]).\n\nWalk the shapes. Each pass produces $h_l^m$, the top block's activation at the last position ([[final-token-activation]]), which is a vector of 768 numbers in the trained model ([[model-specification]]).\n\nTwo passes give two such vectors. Adding them gives one vector of 768 numbers, which is exactly the input [[linear-output-layer-wy]] already expected ([[eq-task-head]]).\n\nSo the head is not told it is being fed a sum. Its shape is identical to the entailment case.\n\nAddition is doing one specific job here. Write the stack as $f$ and the two sentences as $a$ and $b$. The quantity $f([a;b]) + f([b;a])$ is unchanged when you relabel which sentence was called first, because addition commutes.\n\nConcatenating the two vectors would also use both orderings. But it would keep a slot meaning \"the pass where $a$ came first\", widening the head's input to 1536 and putting the arbitrary choice straight back in. Averaging would serve as well as addition, differing by a factor of two that $W_y$ can absorb. The paper names addition and does not compare the alternatives.\n\nBe precise about what became symmetric. Each summand is still produced by an order-sensitive traversal; only the aggregate is invariant. That is sufficient, because the prediction is a function of the aggregate alone.\n\nThe price is two forward and two backward passes per example, paid on [[mrpc]], [[qqp]] and [[sts-b]] ([[semantic-similarity]]) across all three fine-tuning epochs ([[fine-tuning-hyperparameters]]). No ablation isolates the symmetric sum, so what it contributes to those three scores is not measured.", "childId": null, "number": "7.3.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "one-pass-per-candidate", "title": "When the Number of Answers Is Not Known in Advance", "body": "Question answering breaks the pattern somewhere else. Entailment had a fixed number of parts; here the part count is a property of the individual example.\n\nThe template is $[z; q; \\$; a_k]$, built once per candidate ([[qa-and-commonsense-input-transformation]]). Read the punctuation closely. There is one delimiter, and it sits between the question and the answer.\n\nThe passage and the question are run together with no marker at their join. The prose agrees — concatenate the document context and question with each possible answer, delimiter in between — and the multiple-choice panel of the figure draws a single Delim box.\n\nWhy not keep one sequence and give the head one output column per option? Because the option count moves. The [[story-cloze-test]] offers two endings ([[question-answering-and-commonsense-reasoning]]). [[race-dataset]] is built from middle and high school exam passages, and the paper never prints how many choices those questions carry.\n\nA matrix with a column per option would fix that number when the model is built. Running the same [[decoder-only-stack]] $K$ times instead moves the count out of the architecture and into a loop. Handling variable structure inside the network is what [[task-specific-architectures]] would do, and that is the thing being declined.\n\nThe $K$ scores are then normalized by a softmax across sequences. The label space is assembled at run time from whatever candidates the example supplied.\n\nThe head's shape is not stated. The figure draws a Linear box after each candidate's pass, and for a distribution over $K$ options to come out, each box must emit one number, which makes $W_y$ a $768 \\times 1$ matrix.\n\nWhether one such matrix is shared across candidates is left unsaid. A separate matrix per slot would make a candidate's score depend on its position in the list, which is the dependence this design works elsewhere to remove.\n\nThe supervised loss ([[eq-supervised-objective]]) is the log-probability of the correct candidate, so the $K$ passes touch each other only through the shared normalizer. [[table-3]] reports what comes out.", "childId": null, "number": "7.3.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "bill-and-ceiling", "title": "The Bill, and the One Limit Nobody Prices", "body": "Add the passes up per training example. Classification, one. Entailment, one. Similarity, two. Multiple choice, $K$.\n\nThis is a multiplier on fine-tuning compute and on activation memory. It is not a multiplier on parameters: the same weights run every time, which is the whole point of [[task-specific-input-transformations]] and of the [[task-agnostic-model]] claim ([[table-1]]).\n\nIt is also a multiplier on the cheaper of the two stages. Pre-training runs 100 epochs over minibatches of 64 sequences; fine-tuning runs three epochs at batch size 32. Doubling or quadrupling the short stage is a small addition to the total, though the paper never states the comparison itself.\n\nNow the limit. The stack reads 512 tokens ([[context-window-512-tokens]]), a number set during pre-training, where the objective conditions each token on a window of that size ([[language-modeling-objective]]). Position information comes from a trained matrix with one vector per slot ([[learned-position-embeddings]]), so there are no vectors for positions beyond the last one pre-training used.\n\nA comprehension sequence has to fit a whole passage, a question, an answer and the markers inside that. Tokenization pushes the other way: byte-pair encoding with 40,000 merges ([[byte-pair-encoding-gpt1]], part of the [[text-preprocessing-pipeline]]) splits rare words into several units, so the token count runs above the word count.\n\nThe paper does not say whether long passages are truncated, or from which end.\n\nThe silence sits oddly next to the argument being made. RACE is chosen partly for having more reasoning-type questions than [[cnn-and-squad-contrast]], and is described as a fitting test for a model trained on [[long-range-dependencies-gpt1]]. Long range is being claimed as the strength, while the mechanism that would bound it goes unmentioned.\n\nThis is a gap in the reporting, not a demonstrated fault. The RACE numbers were obtained somehow; the paper just does not say how the input was made to fit.", "childId": null, "number": "7.3.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "7.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-the-scoreboard--the-margins": {"id": "n-the-scoreboard--the-margins", "parentId": "n-the-scoreboard", "parentChapterId": "the-margins", "depth": 2, "title": "The Numbers Behind the Margins", "intro": "The parent laid the figures out. This level says what each dataset is actually asking a system to do, what kind of system held the number being subtracted from, and where a blended figure like RACE's 59.0 comes from arithmetically.", "chapters": [{"id": "nli-what-is-asked", "title": "What Entailment Asks, and How It Is Fed In", "body": "All five columns of [[table-2]] score the same task. A system reads two sentences, a premise and a hypothesis, and assigns one of three labels: the premise entails the hypothesis, contradicts it, or leaves it undecided. That is [[natural-language-inference]], which the paper also calls recognizing textual entailment.\n\nSection 4.2 names what makes it hard, and the list is worth reading slowly.\n\nLexical entailment: knowing that one word licenses another, so that a cat on a mat entails an animal on a mat. [[coreference]]: two expressions referring to the same entity, so that whether a pronoun points at the premise's subject or its object can flip the label. Lexical and syntactic ambiguity: a word with two senses, or a phrase that could attach in two places.\n\nNone of that is built into the network. Whatever the model has of it, it has from predicting next tokens on books.\n\nThe feed is identical across all five datasets. Premise tokens and hypothesis tokens are concatenated with a `$` [[delimiter-token]] between them — the [[entailment-input-transformation]] — and the sequence is bracketed by [[start-and-end-tokens]]. The [[final-token-activation]] from the top block goes through [[linear-output-layer-wy]] and softmaxes into three label scores.\n\nSo nothing is reshaped per column. There is no per-dataset [[task-specific-architectures|architecture]] tuned against that benchmark's quirks.\n\nThat matters for reading the spread. A gain of 5.8 points on one column and 0.6 on another came out of the same weights and the same [[three-epochs-fine-tuning|three epochs of fine-tuning]]. Whatever explains the difference sits in the datasets, and in who held the previous number.", "childId": null, "number": "8.2.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "five-sources", "title": "Five Sources, Five Different Ceilings", "body": "Where the sentences come from differs by column, and the paper picks the five for exactly that spread.\n\n[[snli]] pairs are written over image captions, so its premises describe scenes. [[multinli]] mixes transcribed speech, popular fiction and government reports. [[qnli]] is built over Wikipedia articles. [[scitail]] is derived from science exam questions. [[rte]] is drawn from news.\n\nMultiNLI's two reported columns follow from that mix. The matched development set is drawn from genres that also appear in its training data. The mismatched set is drawn from genres held out of training. Mismatched is the lower score for nearly every system in the table, which is why 82.1 and 81.4 sit apart rather than on top of each other.\n\nSize differs as much as source. SNLI is the largest dataset in the whole evaluation at roughly 550k training pairs. RTE is the smallest at 2,490.\n\nNow the other side of each subtraction. SNLI's 89.3 came from five-model ensembles, one of them ESIM — a biLSTM built for inference — with [[elmo-deep-contextualized-word-representations|ELMo]] features added as inputs. That column has absorbed a lot of task-specific work.\n\nQNLI is the opposite case. Of the [[baseline-comparisons]] in the table, only the two multi-task models taken from the [[glue-benchmark]] paper report a QNLI number at all; the rest of the column is dashes. Its 82.3 comes from a general multi-task system, not from something built and re-tuned for QNLI across several papers.\n\nSo the two figures are not measuring the same contest. 0.6 is taken off a column many groups have pushed on. 5.8 is taken off a column almost nobody had entered.", "childId": "n-the-scoreboard--the-margins--five-sources", "number": "8.2.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "why-race", "title": "Why RACE, and What Its Two Splits Say", "body": "[[race-dataset|RACE]] is picked with a stated reason, and the reason is part of the evidence.\n\nIt is reading comprehension assembled from English exam passages, middle school and high school, each with multiple-choice questions written to test whether a student understood the passage. It is the larger half of the [[question-answering-and-commonsense-reasoning]] family.\n\nThe argument for choosing it runs through [[cnn-and-squad-contrast|CNN and SQuAD]]. On those, the answer is something located in the passage — a span, or an entity filling a blank. RACE, the paper says, has been shown to contain more reasoning-type questions than either, which makes it \"the perfect evaluation for our model which is trained to handle long-range contexts\".\n\nRead that plainly. The benchmark is selected because it suits the property being claimed, and the claim about its question mix is cited to other work rather than measured here.\n\nEach candidate answer is scored on its own. Document, question and that one answer are concatenated into a single sequence, and the resulting scores are normalized across candidates by a softmax — the [[qa-and-commonsense-input-transformation]]. Passage plus question plus answer is a long input, which is where [[bookscorpus]] and its [[long-range-contiguous-text]] are meant to pay off.\n\nThe split arithmetic is checkable. The model's two splits are 62.9 and 57.4, whose midpoint is 60.15, yet the combined figure in [[table-3]] is 59.0 — below that midpoint, nearer the high-school number.\n\nSolving $62.9 - 5.5w = 59.0$ gives $w \\approx 0.71$. About seven in ten RACE test questions are high-school ones.\n\nRun the same sum on the previous best row, where 60.2 and 50.3 combine to 53.3, and $w \\approx 0.70$. The two rows agree, which is a small check that the combined column is a weighted blend and nothing stranger.", "childId": null, "number": "8.2.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "story-cloze", "title": "Story Cloze, and the Reading Laid Over It", "body": "[[story-cloze-test|Story Cloze]] hands over a short multi-sentence story and two candidate endings, and asks which one actually finishes it.\n\nTwo options means chance is 50. The live range on that column is not 0 to 100 but 50 to 100. A system at 77.6 has covered a little over half the distance from guessing to perfect; 86.5 covers about three quarters of it.\n\nThe input format is the one RACE gets. Each ending is appended to the story to make its own sequence, both sequences are scored, and the two scores are normalized against each other. [[supervised-fine-tuning]] on the labelled endings trains the single linear layer sitting on top.\n\nThe previous 77.6 is a single system, the Hidden Coherence Model, not an ensemble.\n\nThe paper reads 77.6 against 86.5, together with RACE, as showing the model handles [[long-range-dependencies-gpt1|long-range context]] well. There is a mechanism behind that reading: attention over the whole context is argued to give a more [[transformer-inductive-bias|structured memory]] than a recurrent state carried forward, and the paper elsewhere points at an [[lstm-short-range-limitation|LSTM's shorter effective range]].\n\nBut note what the two numbers do not do. Neither column varies story length, or context length, or anything else that would isolate range as the cause.\n\nA model that had simply absorbed more [[world-knowledge]] about how situations usually end would move that number in the same direction by the same amount.\n\nThe interpretation is laid over the result. It is consistent with the result. It is not separated out by it.", "childId": null, "number": "8.2.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "margins-as-errors", "title": "The Same Margins as Error Reduction", "body": "Every margin so far is a subtraction in accuracy points, which is what the paper means by [[absolute-improvement]]. That treatment gives a point gained at 89 the same weight as a point gained at 53.\n\nHere are the same rows read a second way: what share of the previous system's errors disappeared. This re-expression is not in the paper.\n\nSNLI. The old 89.3 leaves $100 - 89.3 = 10.7$ points of error, and 89.9 leaves 10.1. The gain removes $0.6 / 10.7 \\approx 5.6\\%$ of them.\n\nMultiNLI matched: $1.5 / 19.4 \\approx 7.7\\%$. Mismatched: $1.3 / 19.9 \\approx 6.5\\%$.\n\nSciTail: $5.0 / 16.7 \\approx 30\\%$. QNLI: $5.8 / 17.7 \\approx 33\\%$. Story Cloze: $8.9 / 22.4 \\approx 40\\%$.\n\nRACE overall: $5.7 / 46.7 \\approx 12\\%$, because that column starts with nearly half its questions wrong.\n\nThe RACE splits move closer together under this reading. High school clears $7.1 / 49.7 \\approx 14\\%$ of its errors, middle school $2.7 / 39.8 \\approx 7\\%$ — still twice as much, but not the 2.6-fold gap the raw points suggest.\n\nThis second reading is not neutral either. Near a ceiling, some of the remaining errors are label noise rather than failures a better model could fix, so a small relative gain up there may be worth more than it looks.\n\nNeither reading settles which win is largest. What the paper banks instead is a count: [[state-of-the-art-9-of-12]] across the [[evaluation-task-families]], one [[task-agnostic-model]] behind every entry, and the count treats all nine alike.\n\nNothing in these two tables says why the wins happened. That argument is made separately, by [[layers-transferred]] in [[fig-2]] and by [[ablation-no-pretraining]].", "childId": null, "number": "8.2.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "8.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-which-parts-do-the-work--three-removals": {"id": "n-which-parts-do-the-work--three-removals", "parentId": "n-which-parts-do-the-work", "parentChapterId": "three-removals", "depth": 2, "title": "Three Removals, Eight Datasets", "intro": "The level above gave the three headline deltas and the shape of the grid. This one puts every cell on the page, does the additions the table only prints the results of, and asks what each row can and cannot license.", "chapters": [{"id": "what-the-average-adds-up", "title": "What the Average Column Is Adding Up", "body": "Start with the reference row of [[table-5]], left to right: 45.4, 91.3, 82.3, 82.0, 70.3, 81.8, 88.1, 56.0.\n\nAdd them and you get 597.2. Divide by eight and you get 74.65, which the table prints as 74.7.\n\nThat is the whole operation. Nothing is rescaled first, and no dataset is weighted by how many training examples it has.\n\nThe eight columns do not share a zero. Matthews correlation on [[cola]] sits at 0 for a classifier that guesses, so its usable range runs from 0 to 100. Accuracy on [[sst-2]] sits near 50 for a coin flip on a binary label, and near 33 on three-way [[multinli]]. F1 on the positive class rewards a model that simply answers yes when most pairs carry the positive label, so the floor for [[mrpc]] sits high rather than at zero.\n\nFour different floors, averaged as if they were one scale.\n\nThe practical effect is not that the average is meaningless. It is that the gaps between rows are set mostly by the columns with the most room to move, and those are the ones whose metric bottoms out at zero.\n\nOne more thing about the grid itself. Each of the three lower rows removes exactly one component from the top row, and no row removes two. There is no run with an [[lstm-gpt1]] and no pre-training, and none with neither pre-training nor the auxiliary loss.\n\nSo the table reports three separate effects measured against one reference, and nothing in it measures how they interact.\n\nA last caution on the number. These eight are the [[glue-benchmark]] tasks, but 74.7 is not the 72.8 GLUE score reported in [[table-4]]. They are different quantities and should not be read against each other.", "childId": null, "number": "9.4.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "where-the-collapse-sits", "title": "Where the 14.8 Actually Comes From", "body": "The [[ablation-no-pretraining]] row reads 18.9, 84.0, 79.4, 30.9, 65.5, 75.7, 71.2, 53.8. That sums to 479.4, and $479.4 / 8 = 59.925$, printed as 59.9.\n\nThe headline 14.8 is the difference of two rounded numbers. Unrounded it is $74.65 - 59.925 = 14.725$.\n\nNow take the drops column by column. CoLA falls 26.5. SST-2 falls 7.3. [[mrpc]] falls 2.9. STS-B falls from 82.0 to 30.9, which is 51.1. [[qqp]] falls 4.8, [[multinli|MNLI]] 6.1, [[qnli]] 16.9, and RTE 2.2.\n\nThose eight drops sum to 117.8, and $117.8 / 8 = 14.7$, which is the gap.\n\nSTS-B alone contributes $51.1 / 8 = 6.4$ of it. CoLA contributes 3.3 and QNLI 2.1. Three columns therefore account for 11.8 of the 14.7.\n\nThe remaining five columns together contribute 2.9. Five of the eight datasets move less, in total, than STS-B moves on its own.\n\nWhy STS-B in particular. It is the smallest training set in the evaluation at roughly 5.7k pairs, its target is a continuous similarity score, and its metric is Pearson correlation, whose chance value is 0. Fine-tuning is [[three-epochs-fine-tuning]] at [[fine-tuning-lr-and-batch]], so from a random initialization that is a few hundred updates on a regression target.\n\nAt the other end, RTE moves 2.2 points on 2490 training examples, with both values close to where a majority-class predictor would sit.\n\nThe paper's sentence is that the lack of [[generative-pretraining]] hurts across all the tasks. Every column is down, so the claim holds exactly as written.\n\nThe spread runs from 2.2 to 51.1, so \"across the board\" describes the direction and not the size. The average is largely two columns of [[labeled-data-scarcity]] showing up at once.", "childId": null, "number": "9.4.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "lstm-changes-more-than-one-thing", "title": "The LSTM Row Changes More Than One Thing", "body": "The [[ablation-lstm-substitution]] row reads 30.3, 90.5, 83.2, 71.8, 68.1, 73.7, 81.1, 54.6. That is 553.3, and $553.3 / 8 = 69.16$, printed 69.1.\n\nThe per-column gaps against the full model are 15.1 on CoLA, 0.8 on SST-2, minus 0.9 on MRPC, 10.2 on STS-B, 2.2 on [[qqp]], 8.1 on [[multinli|MNLI]], 7.0 on QNLI and 1.4 on RTE.\n\nCoLA and STS-B again supply 3.2 of the 5.6. The same two columns that dominate the pre-training row dominate this one.\n\nNow the substituted model. It is one recurrent layer of 2048 units. The [[multi-layer-transformer-decoder]] it replaces is [[twelve-layer-depth]] of blocks at [[hidden-state-dimension-768]], each carrying a [[position-wise-feedforward-3072]] inner layer, as fixed by the [[model-specification]].\n\nDepth goes from twelve to one. Width per position goes from 768 to 2048. The mechanism for mixing information across positions changes from attention to a gated recurrent state.\n\nThree changes, one number.\n\nThere is also a missing measurement. The paper reports its transformer's [[token-level-perplexity]] of 18.4 on [[bookscorpus]], and reports nothing equivalent for the LSTM.\n\nSo we cannot tell whether the LSTM transferred worse from a comparable language model, or was simply a worse language model before fine-tuning began.\n\nThe row also keeps the auxiliary loss switched on, and no LSTM row exists without it.\n\nThe [[mrpc]] exception is worth stating precisely. The LSTM's 83.2 beats the full model's 82.3, and it is the only cell in the table where that happens. It does not take the column, because 84.9 elsewhere in the table is higher.\n\nThe architecture argument leans on a second, separate observation in [[fig-2]], where the LSTM's zero-shot scores swing more from checkpoint to checkpoint. The paper offers that as [[transformer-inductive-bias]], and it is a different kind of evidence than the 5.6.", "childId": null, "number": "9.4.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "the-row-that-argues-back", "title": "The Row That Scores Against the Recipe", "body": "The [[ablation-no-auxiliary-objective]] row reads 47.9, 92.0, 84.9, 83.2, 69.8, 81.1, 86.9, 54.4. That is 600.2, and $600.2 / 8 = 75.025$, printed 75.0.\n\nSplit the eight columns by which side wins. Dropping the loss gains 2.5 on CoLA, 0.7 on SST-2, 2.6 on [[mrpc]] and 1.2 on STS-B, for 7.0 points in total.\n\nKeeping it gains 0.5 on [[qqp]], 0.7 on [[multinli|MNLI]], 1.2 on QNLI and 1.6 on [[rte]], for 4.0 points in total.\n\nThat is four columns each way. The three tenths on the average come from the size of the margins rather than from winning more columns: $(7.0 - 4.0)/8 = 0.375$.\n\nThe paper reads the split by dataset size, and for three of the four columns the reading works. QQP and MNLI have hundreds of thousands of training pairs, QNLI has around a hundred thousand, and all three favour keeping [[auxiliary-lm-objective]].\n\nRTE does not fit. It has 2490 examples, the smallest count among the eight, and it favours keeping the loss by the largest margin of the four.\n\nThe paper writes that the trend suggests larger datasets benefit. With four columns on that side and one of them a counterexample, the hedge is doing real work.\n\nThe other missing measurement is the weight itself. [[eq-combined-objective]] scales the language modelling term by $\\lambda$ against [[supervised-objective-l2]], and [[fine-tuning-hyperparameters]] fixes [[auxiliary-objective-weight]] at 0.5.\n\nThe table contains $\\lambda = 0.5$ and $\\lambda = 0$. Two points, no curve, and no run at 0.25 or 1.0.\n\nThe ordering matters too. Every number in [[table-2]] and [[table-3]], across the full [[evaluation-task-families]], was produced at $\\lambda = 0.5$.\n\nSo this row reports on a setting already committed to, rather than choosing one.", "childId": "n-which-parts-do-the-work--three-removals--the-row-that-argues-back", "number": "9.4.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "9.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}});
